---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Button from '../components/Button.astro';
---

<Layout>
  <div class="intro-grid">
    <section class="mission">
      <h2 class="headline">
        Redwoods is a group of <span class="accent">life-long learners</span> committed to supporting
        each other on the journey of design system maturity.
      </h2>
      <p>
        We're creators, not just consumers. Teachers, not just learners. We're here to challenge and
        expand each other's perspectives and to push the design system community forward in
        sustainable, meaningful ways.
      </p>
      <p class="cta-row">
        <Button href="/join" size="sm">Join Redwoods</Button>
        <Button href="#values" variant="secondary" size="sm">Read our values ↓</Button>
      </p>
      <br />
      <blockquote>
        <p>
          “Redwoods is one of the only communities where I feel like I can not only grow in my own
          DS practice by learning from others, but also have a hand in shaping the world of design
          systems at large.”
        </p>
        <footer>~ <cite>ToniAnn</cite>, Redwoods member</footer>
      </blockquote>

      <h2 id="values">Our Values</h2>
      <p>
        Redwoods has three core values that provide the foundation of every interaction we have.
        These are actual, not aspirational. If you're a member, this is who we are, not who we want
        to be. We're not perfect, so we won't always get this right. But we are on a mission to be
        better humans and better practitioners.
      </p>

      <h3>Stay in learning mode.</h3>
      <p>
        Everyone, from junior to senior, has something to teach you. Redwoods encourages continuous
        and open learning, across experience level and discipline.
      </p>

      <h3>Be generous with your knowledge.</h3>
      <p>
        Learning is great, but it's not enough. Redwoods members are creators, not just consumers:
        we analyze, we synthesize, and then we offer our new understanding back to the larger
        community.
      </p>

      <h3>We're in this together.</h3>
      <p>
        Respect is our default. Redwoods is a safe and open community where everyone's experiences
        and perspectives are valued. We're here to support, advise, and guide you throughout your
        systems career.
      </p>
      <blockquote>
        <p>“DS weekly therapy session :)”</p>
        <footer>~ <cite>Pedro</cite>, Redwoods member</footer>
      </blockquote>

      <h2>Why Redwoods?</h2>
      <p>
        Companies don't know how to support their design system teams. We’re often left on an
        island, and the result is design systems that never generate the value we hoped for.
      </p>
      <p>
        We believe deeply in what a design system can do, but systems have to be nurtured. And the
        way you nurture a system is by nurturing the people building it.
      </p>

      <p>
        Redwoods is a committed community for the people doing this work. A safe place to talk about
        the real challenges, support each other through them, and start producing the results we've
        been promising our companies for years.
      </p>

      <blockquote>
        <p>“[Redwoods is] your design system team’s design system team.”</p>
        <footer>~ <cite>Jules</cite>, Redwoods member</footer>
      </blockquote>

      <p>
        And because we're makers, membership is also a commitment to give back, to synthesize what
        we're learning and share it with the wider design system community.
      </p>

      <p class="cta-row">
        <Button href="/join" size="sm">Join Redwoods</Button>
      </p>

      <h2>What membership is like</h2>
      Redwoods runs on a simple rhythm. Here are the ways to show up.

      <h3>Daily</h3>
      We use Slack to hang out, catch up, and connect.
      <h3>Weekly</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Standup:</strong> early each week, share a success, a challenge, and what you're working
          on in #weekly-standup.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Weekly call:</strong> a one-hour video call every Tuesday at noon Eastern. Come to as
          many as you like, and volunteer to facilitate or demo. As we grow we'll add calls and adjust
          for other time zones.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <h3>Monthly</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>All-Redwoods call:</strong> first Friday of each month at noon Eastern. Hear what's
          happening, meet new members, and find ways to get more involved.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Action channels:</strong> each month we run #action- channels for people who want to
          build something together. Every one has a goal, a ~30-day deadline, and an owner. These channels
          co-publish learnings, build tools and templates, create talks, and find other ways to share
          what we synthesize back with the community.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <h3>Quarterly</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>The Redwoods Quarterly:</strong> we occasionally product an online design system event
          covering the recent shifts in the DS space. It gives us something to work toward and a stage
          to share our biggest takeaways. A subset of Redwoods membership presents and everyone else attends
          free.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <h3>Also included</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>Discount codes for industry events and online courses.</li>
        <li>A Collab Figma seat as part of your membership.</li>
        <li>
          Behind-the-scenes access to <a href="https://bencallahan.com/the-question">The Question</a
          >, a live design system research show.
        </li>
        <li>Discounts on individual coaching and strategy sessions.<br /></li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="values">
      <br />
      <h2>Is Redwoods for you?</h2>

      Redwoods is structured to prioritize anyone who:
      <ul>
        <li>Aligns with our values</li>
        <li>Wants to grow in their craft</li>
        <li>Wants to make a sustainable impact on the industry</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        We have researchers, product managers, designers, ux-ers, developers...you name it. If
        you’re a part-time design system team of one, or one of 30 systems practitioners working at
        enterprise scale, Redwoods has a place for you.
      </p>

      <p>
        Consultants and folks at product companies are welcome too, just know this is a space for
        mutual support, not sales.

        <blockquote>
          <p>
            “The community of Redwoods is made of great people from whom I’ve learned so much
            through the years.”
          </p>
          <footer>~ <cite>Robin</cite>, Redwoods member</footer>
        </blockquote>

        <h2 id="join">Join Redwoods</h2>

        <p>
          Membership is available monthly or yearly, and many organizations cover the cost through a
          training or education budget.
        </p>
        <p><strong>What you get:</strong></p>
        <ul>
          <li>Daily Slack conversations</li>
          <li>Weekly video calls</li>
          <li>Presentation coaching</li>
          <li>Co-publishing opportunities</li>
          <li>Design system peer reviews</li>
          <li>Member showcases</li>
          <li>Research collaborations</li>
          <li>Focused problem solving</li>
          <li>Free access to The Redwoods Quarterly</li>
          <li>Discounts to industry events and online courses</li>
          <li>A Collab Figma seat</li>
          <li>New friends, and much more</li>
        </ul>
        <p class="cta-row">
          <Button href="/join" size="sm">Join Monthly</Button>
          <Button href="/join" variant="secondary" size="sm">Join Yearly</Button>
        </p>

        <p><a href="https://bencallahan.com/connect">Questions? Reach out.</a></p>

        <h2>The history of Redwoods</h2>

        <p>
          I was in Muir Woods a few years ago, a coastal redwood forest, and a genuinely spiritual
          place to visit. The ground is covered in needles, there's no noise, the air is pure, and
          you're surrounded by trees as tall as the Statue of Liberty. It's breathtaking.
        </p>
        <p>
          When people first settled the area, they cut these trees down for lumber but would leave a
          few standing. Inevitably, after a short time, the trees left behind would fall over.
        </p>
        <p>
          Even though these trees are hundreds of feet tall, their roots only go down about ten
          feet. But those roots grow outward, as much as 80 feet, and intertwine with the roots of
          every other redwood around them. These trees hold each other up. That's what Redwoods is.
          A place to hold each other up.
        </p>
        <p>
          Beyond this, if you read the writings of John Muir, you’ll recognize that he saw the
          interconnectedness present in a Redwood forest:
        </p>

        <blockquote>
          <p>
            “When one tugs at a single thing in nature, he finds it attached to the rest of the
            world.”
          </p>
          <footer>― <cite>John Muir</cite></footer>
        </blockquote>

        <p>
          I started Redwoods because I needed that connection. If you’re looking for it too, perhaps
          Redwoods is for you.
        </p>
        <p>Ben Callahan,<br />Founder of Redwoods</p>
      </p>
    </section>
  </div>

  <style>
    .headline {
      margin-block-start: 0;
      font-size: var(--font-size-hero);
    }

    /* Grows to display size once the main column itself is wide, not the
     viewport — see the container on .main-wrap in Page.astro. */
    @container main (min-width: 640px) {
      .headline {
        font-size: var(--font-size-hero-max);
      }
    }

    .accent {
      color: var(--color-green);
    }

    .cta-row {
      display: flex;
      flex-wrap: wrap;
      gap: var(--space-sm);
    }

    .intro-grid {
      margin-block-start: var(--space-xl);
    }

    /* Mobile: sections just stack, so the second one needs its own gap
       above it in place of the grid/column gap used at wider widths. */
    .intro-grid > .values {
      margin-block-start: var(--space-xl);
    }

    .values h2:first-of-type {
      margin-block-start: 0;
    }

    .values h3 {
      font-size: var(--font-size-lg);
    }

    /* Wide: a native CSS multi-column flow instead of a fixed 2-col grid, so
       the two halves self-balance by height as copy is added or trimmed
       rather than leaving one column trailing off in whitespace. Section
       boundaries no longer align with the column break, so keep headings
       attached to what follows them and keep lists from splitting mid-way. */
    @container main (min-width: 640px) {
      .intro-grid {
        column-count: 2;
        column-gap: var(--space-xl);
      }

      .intro-grid > .values {
        margin-block-start: 0;
      }

      .intro-grid h2,
      .intro-grid h3 {
        break-after: avoid;
      }

      .intro-grid ul {
        break-inside: avoid;
      }
    }
  </style>
</Layout>
